\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Difference in normalised confusion matrix of the model trained on synthetic data and \emph  {pop} data versus just \emph  {pop} data. Training on synthetic data discourages the model from predicting \texttt  {X} chords, which are not present in the synthetic data. Recall on \texttt  {min7} and \texttt  {dim} qualities increases. However, recall on \texttt  {hdim7} and \texttt  {min6} worsens. In general, the model predicts \texttt  {maj} less often for rarer qualities. The synthetic data alters many of the predictions on \texttt  {7} qualities from \texttt  {min} to \texttt  {maj}. Something similar happens with \texttt  {min7} qualities. It is hard to say which model is better. Indeed, their overall accuracies are the same.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cm_synthetic_data}{{1}{5}{}{}{}}
